{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Populate `RemoteRef` on the inbound path, and correct what it actually protects\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`RemoteRef` — the backend-neutral identity value `{vendor, instance, remote_id}` defined at\n`_backend.py:81` — is **never constructed in production code**. Re-verified against the current\ntree, not taken from the original report: in `src/` there is only the frozen dataclass, its\ndocstring, and one comment in `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:206`. Every construction is in\ntests (`backend_support.py:228`, `test_backend_contract.py:96-97`).\n\nIts `instance` field is documented as naming \"the concrete deployment (e.g. a Jira site / project\nhost) so two instances of the same vendor never collide\". Nothing populates it, so that sentence\ndescribes an intention rather than a behaviour.\n\nThis surfaced in J7 [rebar:b4e3-1de0-3188-40a1], whose AC required asserting \"a populated\n`RemoteRef.instance`\" on a DC-created ticket. That assertion was not satisfiable against any\nreconcile pass, so J7's AC was narrowed to the provenance that IS observable (creation_channel +\nlocal-id prefix) and this ticket was filed for the remainder.\n\n## The decision this plan makes\n\nThe original draft said \"needs design, not just implementation\" and left the `instance` source\nopen. It is decided here, because a plan that defers its central choice cannot be implemented from.\n\n**`instance` is derived from the CONFIGURED base URL** — normalised (scheme dropped, host\nlowercased, default port dropped, trailing slash dropped, context path retained, since DC is\ncommonly served at `/jira`). It is available wherever a backend is constructed\n(`JiraDataCenterSettings.url`, `settings.py:45`, itself `reconciler.base_url`), needs no extra REST\nround-trip, and requires no operator action.\n\n**The stability objection is real and is answered by SCOPE, not by cleverness.** The draft warned\nthat \"if `instance` is derived from a mutable base URL, an operator moving their instance silently\nre-identifies every ticket\". That is true of a PERSISTED key. It is not true here, and the reason\nis concrete: `RemoteRef` is not persisted by this story and is not a binding key. Bindings already\nkey on the issue's IMMUTABLE NUMERIC ID — [rebar:7c26-4ac8-04a3-440e] changed exactly that, so a\nmoved or re-keyed issue survives. `RemoteRef` is in-memory disambiguation within a pass. A URL\nchange therefore re-labels nothing on disk.\n**This story does NOT make it a persisted key, and says so in the ADR.** Any future story that\npersists a `RemoteRef` inherits the stability problem and must solve it there (the candidate is an\noperator-set opaque id, or the instance's own server id — neither is needed for in-memory use, and\nadding either now would be unused configuration).\n\n## THE CORRECTION THIS TICKET ACTUALLY DELIVERS — the collision claim is FALSE as written\n\nVerified in source rather than reasoned about, and it changes the ticket's conclusion:\n\n* `vendor` ALREADY separates the deployments. Cloud's backend sets `vendor = \"jira\"`\n  (`adapters/jira/backend.py:142`); DC's sets `vendor = \"jira-datacenter\"`\n  (`adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:200`). So Cloud-vs-DC never needed `instance`; what\n  `instance` disambiguates is TWO DEPLOYMENTS OF THE SAME VENDOR — e.g. two DC instances.\n* **Populating `instance` does NOT prevent the collision the docstring claims it prevents.** The\n  colliding value is the LOCAL TICKET ID, and `_jira_key_to_local_id`\n  (`inbound_translate.py:120-124`) is `\"jira-\" + jira_key.lower()` — it takes the Jira key and\n  NOTHING else. Two DC deployments that each have a project `DIG` both mint `jira-dig-123` for\n  their own issue 123, whatever `RemoteRef.instance` says. The field cannot fix that, because\n  nothing consults it when the local id is minted.\n\nSo AC4 of this ticket (\"e369's shared-identity claim is either satisfied or its ADR amended to\nmatch reality\") resolves to **AMEND THE ADR**. Making the local id instance-aware would change the\nid scheme for every existing Jira-sourced ticket — a breaking, store-wide migration that is not\nthis ticket and must not be smuggled into it.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Add a small pure helper that normalises a base URL to an `instance` string, with its own unit\n   tests (this is the only logic; everything else is wiring).\n2. Construct a `RemoteRef` on the INBOUND path where an issue is translated, from\n   `backend.vendor`, the derived `instance`, and the Jira key as `remote_id`.\n3. Amend the ADR (and `RemoteRef`'s own docstring, which is where a reader will look first) to\n   state what `instance` does and does not protect, and to record that it is not persisted.\n\n**Do not add an operator-facing config key for this.** An unused setting is a maintenance liability\nand the derived value needs no input.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `rebar_reconciler/_backend.py` — `RemoteRef` docstring correction only; NO field change (it is a\n  frozen value type with a passing contract test, and changing its shape is out of scope).\n- the inbound translation path — construct the `RemoteRef`.\n- a new pure `instance`-normalisation helper + unit tests.\n- the ADR amended per the section above.\n\n**NOT in scope:** changing `_jira_key_to_local_id` or the local-id scheme (a breaking store-wide\nmigration); persisting `RemoteRef` anywhere; any new config key; `RemoteRef.instance` for the Cloud\nadapter beyond whatever falls out of using the same helper.\n\n## Testing\n\n* the normalisation helper is unit-tested directly: two DIFFERENT deployment URLs give DIFFERENT\n  `instance` values, and two spellings of the SAME deployment (trailing slash, upper-case host,\n  explicit default port) give the SAME one. The second half is the one that matters — a helper that\n  merely returns the URL passes the first half trivially.\n* a reconcile pass constructs a `RemoteRef` whose `instance` is non-empty, asserted on the value\n  the pass actually built rather than on a re-derivation of it.\n* two backends configured at different base URLs produce `RemoteRef`s that are NOT equal, and two\n  configured at the same URL produce equal ones (`RemoteRef` is frozen and value-equal, which\n  `test_backend_contract.py:96-97` already pins).\n* a REGRESSION GUARD for the correction above: a test asserting that two deployments sharing a\n  project key DO still mint the same local id, with a docstring naming this as known and bounded.\n  Pinning the limitation stops a later reader assuming `instance` solved it.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a pure helper derives `instance` from a base URL; different deployments differ, and\n      equivalent spellings of one deployment (trailing slash / case / default port) agree\n- [ ] a reconcile pass CONSTRUCTS a `RemoteRef` with a populated, non-empty `instance`, asserted\n      against the object the pass built\n- [ ] two backends at different base URLs yield unequal `RemoteRef`s for the same `remote_id`\n- [ ] the chosen `instance` source is documented, INCLUDING what happens when the base URL changes,\n      and including that this story does not persist a `RemoteRef` (so a URL change re-labels\n      nothing on disk, because bindings key on the immutable numeric id per 7c26)\n- [ ] the ADR and `RemoteRef`'s docstring are AMENDED to state that `instance` does NOT prevent\n      local-id collision between two same-vendor deployments — `_jira_key_to_local_id` derives the\n      id from the Jira key alone — and that `vendor` already separates Cloud from DC\n- [ ] a test pins that known limitation, so it cannot be silently assumed fixed\n- [ ] no new operator-facing config key is introduced, and `_jira_key_to_local_id` is unchanged"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785622524063253001,"uuid":"61ec1831-e003-443a-9172-cb1ab6e77e66"}